/-!
# Admissible floor sections do not depend on the crepant dlt model

Let `(X, B)` and `(Y, C)` be crepant birational rational `ℚ`-factorial projective complex pairs
of dimension at most three. Each is dlt, or more generally log canonical with
`(X, B - δ⌊B⌋)` klt for some `δ > 0` (a central lc model whose floor-lowering pair is klt).
Let `m` be an even integer with `m(K_X + B)` and `m(K_Y + C)` Cartier, and let
`S_X = ⌊B⌋`, `S_Y = ⌊C⌋` be the reduced floors. Then the spaces
`H⁰(S_X, m(K_X + B)|_{S_X})` and `H⁰(S_Y, m(K_Y + C)|_{S_Y})` are canonically identified. The
identification is read on a common model `X ← W → Y`. Let `T` be the reduced divisor of `W`
whose components are the coefficient-one components of the common crepant boundary `B_W`; it
includes the exceptional places of log discrepancy zero. A floor section on `X` and a floor
section on `Y` correspond when their restrictions to every component of `T` agree. This
identification preserves admissibility of the section systems induced on the normal floor
components and their strata when both pairs are dlt.

The proof applies the all-zero-floor pushforward lemma
(`MMP.Fourfold.IsLogResolution.bijOn_idealSheaf_divisorialSubmodule_and_pushforward_floor`) on a
common log resolution, which gives `𝒪_{S_X} = q_* 𝒪_T` and `𝒪_{S_Y} = q'_* 𝒪_T`. With the
projection formula, this yields the identification. Admissibility is then compared stratum by
stratum, by the alternating reduction along crepant SNC stratum maps
(`MMP.Fourfold.IsSNCSubboundaryPair.isSNCStratum_image_and_exists_bijective_restriction`).

## Main results

* `MMP.Fourfold.exists_linearMap_bijOn_floorSections_of_isCrepantBoundary`.

## Implementation notes

* The normal components of the floor are given as data `C_P`, `j_P`, `B_{C_P}`, indexed by the
  prime divisors `P` with `⌊coeff_P B⌋ ≠ 0`. Each `j_P` is the adjunction step onto `P` with its
  different (`MMP.Fourfold.IsAdjunctionStep`).
* A section of `m(K_X + B)|_{S_X}` is recorded on these components, as a family
  `t_P ∈ H⁰(C_P, m(K_{C_P} + B_{C_P}))`. Such a family is a floor section when it is locally
  the restriction of a section of the ambient sheaf. That is, every point of `X` has an open
  neighbourhood `U` and a section `τ` of `𝒪_X(mK_X + ⌊mB⌋)` over `U` whose Poincaré residue
  (`MMP.Fourfold.IsPoincareResidue`) along every `C_P` meeting `U` is `t_P`.
* This describes `H⁰(S_X, m(K_X + B)|_{S_X})` because `S_X` is reduced, `m(K_X + B)` is
  invertible, and its restriction to `C_P` is `m(K_{C_P} + B_{C_P})` through the residue.
* The crepant birational equivalence is a common model: a normal variety `W` with proper
  birational morphisms `p : W ⟶ X` and `q : W ⟶ Y` over `ℂ`, and one `ℚ`-divisor `B_W` that is
  the crepant boundary (`MMP.Mori.IsCrepantBoundary`) of both `(X, B)` and `(Y, C)`.
* The identification `e` is pinned on `W`. Let `t` be a floor section, let `τ` present `t`
  near a point and `τ'` present `e t` near a point, and let `E` be a coefficient-one prime divisor
  of `B_W` whose generic point lies over both neighbourhoods. Then `p^*τ` and `q^*τ'` have the same
  Poincaré residue along `E`. The strict transforms of the floor components of `Y` are among these
  `E`, so this determines `e t`.
* The map `e` is a `ℂ`-linear map between the full spaces of component families. Only its
  restriction to floor sections is constrained, and that restriction is a bijection onto the
  floor sections of `Y`.
* Admissibility is `MMP.Fourfold.admissibleSections` for any residue restriction structure on
  the floor collections in degree `m`. Such a structure contains the Cartier condition on
  stratum adjoints, which the source puts in "Cartier adjoint degree".

## References

* [J. Kollár et al., *Flips and abundance for algebraic threefolds*][KollarFlips]
-/

@[expose] public section

